\title{Psychometric DIF Analysis Reveals 31\% Temporal Item Instability\\in MMLU: A Large-Scale Characterization of Benchmark Measurement Properties}

\author{Anonymous}
\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

%% ============================================================
%%  ABSTRACT
%% ============================================================
\begin{abstract}
When we compare language model scores across time, we implicitly assume that benchmark items function identically for all model cohorts.
We test this assumption by applying Mantel--Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) analysis to the METABENCH response matrix (5{,}227 models $\times$ 12{,}508 MMLU items)---the largest-scale DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks to date.
We find that 31.3\% of items exhibit large DIF (ETS Category~C, $|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}|\!\ge\!1.5$) between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts, robust across thresholds (10--49\% at $|\Delta|\in[1.0,2.5]$), not explained by domain-level capability improvement ($\le$7.6\%), and architecturally homogeneous (99.98\% decoder-only).
We show that the field's standard web-overlap contamination labels cannot validate item-level differential detectors (enrichment$\approx$1.0) and propose a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching that eliminates false-positive inflation ($\Delta$FPR$=$0.000).
Despite 31\% instability, MMLU model rankings are massively redundant ($\rho\!\approx\!0.997$), and DIF items serve as a diagnostic lens---identifying \emph{which} capability dimensions shift across generations rather than flagging ``bad'' items.
Cross-benchmark replication on GSM8K (1{,}319 items $\times$ 6{,}702 models) confirms framework portability ($\Delta$TPR$=+$0.818) and shows DIF items resist contamination-based gains ($r\!=\!-0.478$, $p\!=\!0.005$).
\end{abstract}

%% ============================================================
%%  1  INTRODUCTION
%% ============================================================
\section{Introduction}

Benchmarks such as MMLU~\citep{hendrycks2021measuring} are the primary yardstick for comparing language models.
Temporal comparisons---``GPT-4 surpasses GPT-3.5 on MMLU''---rest on an untested assumption: that every item measures the same construct with the same difficulty for every model cohort.
Psychometrics has a mature tool for evaluating exactly this assumption: \emph{Differential Item Functioning} (DIF) analysis, which detects items whose difficulty changes across groups after controlling for overall ability~\citep{holland1988differential}.

We apply the Mantel--Haenszel (MH) DIF procedure---unchanged since 1988---to the METABENCH response matrix comprising 5{,}227 models and 12{,}508 MMLU items.
We do \emph{not} claim algorithmic novelty; MH-DIF is a textbook method.
Our contributions are empirical:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Temporal instability at scale.}
    31.3\% of MMLU items are ETS Category~C between 2023 and 2024-H1 cohorts.
    This is not a statistical artifact (random-split sanity: 0.18\%), not driven by capability improvement ($\le$7.6\%), and robust to threshold choice.
    \item \textbf{Ground-truth meta-critique.}
    Web-overlap contamination labels (e.g., Li et al.) measure global exposure, not differential contamination.
    Enrichment$\approx$1.0 is the expected outcome, not method failure.
    \item \textbf{Semi-synthetic validation framework.}
    $\Delta$TPR methodology with cross-subject matching ($\Delta$FPR$=$0.000).
    Portable to GSM8K ($\Delta$TPR$=+$0.818).
    \item \textbf{DIF as diagnostic lens.}
    Despite 31\% instability, rankings are stable ($\rho\!\approx\!0.997$).
    DIF items are the \emph{least} disruptive non-random removal strategy ($z\!=\!-9.5$ vs.\ accuracy-gap $z\!=\!-157$), isolating genuine differential functioning.
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{Key framing.}
Instability $\neq$ invalidity.
DIF flags items whose measurement properties changed---a diagnostic signal, not a quality verdict.
MMLU rankings survive 31\% item removal because the benchmark is massively redundant.
DIF tells us \emph{what changed}, not ``what is broken.''

%% ============================================================
%%  2  RELATED WORK
%% ============================================================
\section{Related Work}

\paragraph{LLM benchmark contamination.}
Web-overlap and $n$-gram methods~\citep{li2024task} identify publicly available items but do not assess \emph{differential} item functioning.
ConStat~\citep{constat2024} uses perturbation testing; Min-K\%~\citep{shi2024detecting} uses token-level statistics.
Both operate per-model, unlike DIF which is a group-level method.

\paragraph{Differential Item Functioning.}
The MH procedure~\citep{mantel1959statistical,holland1988differential} tests whether item difficulty differs between focal and reference groups after conditioning on total score.
ETS classifies items as A ($|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}|<1.0$), B ($1.0$--$1.5$), or C ($\ge\!1.5$).
DIF analysis has been standard in educational testing for decades.

\paragraph{IRT and LLM evaluation.}
Item Response Theory has been applied to LLM evaluation~\citep{irt_llm2024}, though IRT model fit for multidimensional benchmarks remains challenging.

%% ============================================================
%%  3  METHOD
%% ============================================================
\section{Method}

\subsection{Mantel--Haenszel DIF}

Given a binary response matrix $\mathbf{X}\in\{0,1\}^{N\times J}$ ($N$ models, $J$ items), we partition models into a \emph{reference} group (2023 cohort) and a \emph{focal} group (2024-H1 cohort).
For each item $j$ and total-score stratum $k$, the MH statistic tests the null hypothesis that the item--group odds ratio is 1.
The effect size $\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}$ (in ETS delta units) quantifies the magnitude.
Items with $|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}|\ge 1.5$ and statistically significant MH $\chi^2$ are classified as ETS Category~C.

\paragraph{IRT fit caveat.}
MH conditions on total score as an ability proxy.
On MMLU (57 subjects), the unidimensional IRT model is misspecified: 98\% of models show person-fit $|L_z|>2$.
Total score collapses a multidimensional ability space into a single composite.
This is a known limitation throughout the paper.

\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation Framework}

To evaluate DIF detection power with known ground truth, we inject controlled contamination into the real METABENCH response matrix:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Select $n_{\mathrm{clean}}$ items with no DIF signal as ``clean'' items.
    \item For each ``injected'' item, flip a fraction $p$ of focal-group incorrect responses to correct, simulating differential contamination.
    \item Run MH-DIF on the modified matrix and measure $\Delta\text{TPR} = \text{TPR}_{\text{injected}} - \text{TPR}_{\text{baseline}}$.
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{Cross-subject matching.}
Standard MH uses total score (all subjects) as matching variable.
When contamination affects the matching variable itself, FPR is inflated.
We propose using total score on \emph{other} subjects as the matching variable, eliminating this confound.

\subsection{Comparison Methods}

\paragraph{ConStat perturbation testing.}
We reformulate 220 MMLU items and measure detection rate via paired accuracy drop.

\paragraph{DVD generation variance.}
For 12 models $\times$ 200 items $\times$ 50 generations, we detect bimodal response variance as a contamination signal.

\paragraph{Ensemble fusion.}
Majority vote and weighted fusion of DIF + ConStat + DVD signals.

%% ============================================================
%%  4  EXPERIMENTS
%% ============================================================
\section{Experiments}

\subsection{Experiment 1: Temporal DIF on MMLU}
\label{sec:exp1}

\paragraph{Setup.}
5{,}227 models $\times$ 12{,}508 MMLU items from METABENCH.
Reference: models released before 2024; Focal: 2024-H1.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Temporal DIF results on MMLU. C\% = fraction of items classified ETS Category~C. Enrichment measures overlap with Li et al.\ web-overlap contamination labels. Random-split sanity check confirms non-trivial signal.}
\label{tab:temporal_dif}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
\textbf{Metric} & \textbf{Value} \\
\midrule
Temporal C\% & 31.3\% \\
Li-clean FPR & 0.318 \\
Enrichment (DIF $\cap$ Li-contaminated) & 0.96 \\
Random-split sanity C\% & 0.18\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Cohort comparison. Temporal vs.\ ability-split DIF and within-family (Llama-2 vs.\ Llama-3) DIF. Domain-level Spearman $\rho=0.307$ ($p=0.02$) between C\% and accuracy improvement.}
\label{tab:cohort}
\begin{tabular}{lc}
\toprule
\textbf{Cohort split} & \textbf{C\%} \\
\midrule
Temporal (2023 vs.\ 2024-H1) & 31.3\% \\
Ability-matched & 2.9\% \\
Within-family (Llama-2 vs.\ Llama-3) & 61.3\% \\
Domain $\rho$ (C\% $\sim$ accuracy gain) & 0.307 ($p=0.02$) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

31.3\% of items show large DIF between temporal cohorts.
The ability-matched split (2.9\%) shows that the effect is predominantly temporal, not ability-driven.
Within-family comparison (Llama-2 vs.\ Llama-3) reaches 61.3\%, consistent with rapid within-family capability shifts.
Enrichment with web-overlap labels is $\approx$1.0, indicating DIF flags and web-overlap labels capture different phenomena.
The random-split sanity check (0.18\%) confirms the signal is not statistical noise.

\paragraph{IRT person-fit.}
98\% of models show $|L_z|>2$ under a unidimensional IRT model, confirming that MMLU's 57-subject structure violates 1D assumptions.
The MH procedure's conditioning on total score is therefore an approximation to multidimensional ability matching.

\subsection{Experiment 2: Architecture Stratification}
\label{sec:arch}

METABENCH MMLU comprises 5{,}226 decoder-only models and 1 non-decoder model.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Architecture stratification. Removing the single non-decoder model produces identical DIF results.}
\label{tab:arch}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Population} & \textbf{C\%} & \textbf{Jaccard} & \textbf{RMSEA} & \textbf{CFI} \\
\midrule
Full ($N=5{,}227$) & 31.3\% & --- & 0.087 & 0.73 \\
Decoder-only ($N=5{,}226$) & 31.3\% & 1.00 & 0.087 & 0.73 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Architecture heterogeneity is a non-issue: Jaccard similarity between full and decoder-only DIF flags is 1.0.
IRT fit remains poor (RMSEA$=$0.087, CFI$=$0.73, $|L_z|>2$: 97.8\%), confirming that misfit stems from multidimensionality, not architecture mixing.

\subsection{Experiment 3: Semi-Synthetic Contamination Validation}
\label{sec:semisyn}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Semi-synthetic TPR/FPR across injection rates $p$. Temporal-external matching. Baseline FPR = 0.314 reflects the underlying DIF rate prior to injection.}
\label{tab:semisyn}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Injection rate $p$} & \textbf{TPR} & \textbf{$\Delta$TPR} & \textbf{FPR} \\
\midrule
0.0 (baseline) & 0.326 & --- & 0.314 \\
0.3 & 0.548 & +0.222 & --- \\
0.5 & 0.834 & +0.508 & --- \\
1.0 & 1.000 & +0.674 & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Standard vs.\ cross-subject matching. Cross-subject matching eliminates FPR inflation ($\Delta$FPR$=$0.000). Standard matching shows inflation from 0.298 to 0.616 at $p=1.0$.}
\label{tab:matching}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
\textbf{Matching strategy} & \textbf{$\Delta$FPR at $p=1.0$} \\
\midrule
Standard (all subjects) & $0.298 \to 0.616$ (+0.318) \\
Cross-subject (other subjects) & 0.000 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

$\Delta$TPR shows a clear dose-response: detection power increases monotonically with injection rate.
At $p=0.5$, $\Delta$TPR$=+$0.508, confirming DIF can detect differential contamination above baseline.
Cross-subject matching ($\Delta$FPR$=$0.000) is a key methodological contribution, eliminating the matching-variable contamination artifact.

\paragraph{Ability-split results.}
Under ability-based matching, TPR at $p=1.0$ is 0.80, but FPR rises to 0.493---confirming that ability-based cohorts confound the matching variable more severely.

\subsection{Experiment 4: Cross-Benchmark Replication (GSM8K)}
\label{sec:gsm8k}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{GSM8K DIF analysis (1{,}319 items, $N_{\mathrm{ref}}=1{,}017$, $N_{\mathrm{foc}}=804$) and GSM1K contamination gap correlation ($N=33$ models).}
\label{tab:gsm8k}
\begin{tabular}{lc}
\toprule
\textbf{Metric} & \textbf{Value} \\
\midrule
GSM8K standard temporal C\% & 18.9\% \\
GSM8K external C\% & 47.4\% \\
GSM8K sanity C\% & 0.3\% \\
Semi-synthetic $\Delta$TPR ($p=1.0$) & 0.818 \\
\midrule
GSM1K Pearson $r$ & $-0.478$ ($p=0.005$) \\
GSM1K Spearman $\rho$ & $-0.403$ ($p=0.020$) \\
GSM1K BCa CI (Pearson) & $[-0.714, -0.155]$ \\
GSM1K partial $r$ (controlling accuracy) & $-0.385$ ($p=0.030$) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

GSM8K replication confirms framework portability.
Semi-synthetic $\Delta$TPR$=+$0.818 on a unidimensional math benchmark demonstrates strong detection power when IRT assumptions are better satisfied.
GSM1K contamination gap correlates negatively with DIF flags ($r=-0.478$, $p=0.005$): models with larger contamination gaps show lower performance on DIF-flagged items.
This is consistent with DIF items capturing genuine capability rather than memorization-based gains.

\paragraph{Caveats.}
$N=33$ models limits statistical power; BCa CI upper bound $|r|=0.155$ admits a small true effect.
GSM8K is single-domain (math), so generalization to MMLU's 57-subject multidimensionality is not direct.

\subsection{Experiment 5: RL Model Robustness}
\label{sec:rl}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{AUROC comparison across detection methods on reasoning-RL models ($N=13$ GRPO/PPO models within a $N=477$ alignment-RL cohort). Bootstrap 95\% CI in brackets.}
\label{tab:rl_auroc}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{AUROC} & \textbf{95\% CI} \\
\midrule
DIF & 0.58 & $[0.41, 0.73]$ \\
ConStat & 0.54 & --- \\
Min-K\% & 0.51 & --- \\
\midrule
DeLong $p$ (DIF vs.\ ConStat) & 0.38 & \\
TOST equivalence $p$ & 0.22 & \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{DIF C\% by cohort type and power analysis at varying injection rates.}
\label{tab:rl_power}
\begin{tabular}{lc}
\toprule
\textbf{Cohort} & \textbf{C\%} \\
\midrule
SFT baseline & 30.2\% \\
Alignment-RL ($N=477$) & 31.9\% \\
GRPO-specific ($N=13$) & 34.8\% \\
Jaccard (SFT vs.\ GRPO) & 0.71 \\
\midrule
\textbf{Injection rate $p$} & \textbf{Power (TPR)} \\
\midrule
0.4 & 0.12 \\
0.7 & 0.43 \\
1.0 & 0.68 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

With only 13 reasoning-RL models, this analysis is exploratory.
C\% is comparable across SFT (30.2\%), alignment-RL (31.9\%), and GRPO-specific (34.8\%) cohorts, and item-level overlap is high (Jaccard$=$0.71).
No method achieves significant AUROC advantage (DeLong $p=0.38$); all CIs overlap chance.
Power is limited: at $p=0.4$, TPR$=$0.12.
DIF is a group-level method and cannot provide per-model detection---a structural limitation acknowledged but not resolved.

\subsection{Experiment 6: ConStat Perturbation Evaluation}
\label{sec:constat}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{ConStat vs.\ DIF detection comparison on 220 reformulated MMLU items.}
\label{tab:constat}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
\textbf{Metric} & \textbf{ConStat} & \textbf{DIF} \\
\midrule
Detection rate (non-RL) & 0.41 & --- \\
Detection rate (RL) & 0.08 & --- \\
FDR-controlled rate & 0.23 & --- \\
FPR & 0.067 & 0.318 \\
Unique detections & 158 & 892 \\
Overlap (\%) & \multicolumn{2}{c}{12.7\%} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

ConStat achieves controlled FPR (0.067) by design via FDR correction, substantially lower than DIF's 0.318 on Li-clean items.
Detection overlap is only 12.7\%, suggesting the methods capture different phenomena: ConStat detects perturbation sensitivity (per-model), while DIF detects group-level item functioning changes.
ConStat degrades sharply on RL models (0.08 vs.\ 0.41 on non-RL), consistent with RL training producing more robust response patterns.

\subsection{Experiment 7: DVD Generation Variance Evaluation}
\label{sec:dvd}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{DVD detection rates across 12 models $\times$ 200 items $\times$ 50 generations per item.}
\label{tab:dvd}
\begin{tabular}{lc}
\toprule
\textbf{Metric} & \textbf{Value} \\
\midrule
Bimodal detection rate & 0.14 \\
Variance-based detection rate & 0.22 \\
DVD vs.\ DIF overlap & 0.09 \\
DVD FPR & 0.04 \\
GPU hours & 65 \\
Variant contamination detected & 31 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

DVD achieves very low FPR (0.04) but limited detection rate (bimodal: 14\%, variance: 22\%).
Overlap with DIF is only 9\%, again confirming orthogonality.
The method requires 120K inference generations (65 GPU hours), limiting scalability.
DVD may be better positioned as a follow-up diagnostic on flagged items rather than a primary detector.

\subsection{Experiment 8: Ensemble Fusion}
\label{sec:ensemble}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Ensemble configurations combining DIF, ConStat, and DVD signals.}
\label{tab:ensemble_config}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
\textbf{Configuration} & \textbf{Precision} & \textbf{Recall} \\
\midrule
Majority vote & 0.38 & 0.09 \\
Weighted fusion & 0.44 & 0.15 \\
Ensemble FPR & \multicolumn{2}{c}{0.087} \\
$\Delta F_1$ (optimized $-$ uniform) & \multicolumn{2}{c}{0.02} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Cross-validation fold weights for weighted fusion. High variance (std/mean $>$ 0.6) indicates instability.}
\label{tab:ensemble_weights}
\begin{tabular}{lc}
\toprule
\textbf{Signal} & \textbf{Weight (mean)} \\
\midrule
DIF & 0.28 \\
ConStat & 0.51 \\
DVD & 0.21 \\
\midrule
Weight stability & std/mean $>$ 0.6 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Ensemble results are a negative finding.
Majority vote achieves only 9\% recall; weighted fusion reaches 15\%.
The marginal benefit of optimization over uniform weights ($\Delta F_1 = 0.02$) is negligible.
Cross-validation weights are unstable (std/mean $>$ 0.6), and DIF receives the lowest weight (0.28) among the three signals.
Low pairwise overlaps ($<$15\%) between methods predict this failure: combining orthogonal weak signals does not produce a coherent detector.
We report this as an informative null result.

\subsection{Experiment 9: Bifactor IRT Equating Pilot}
\label{sec:bifactor}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Bifactor IRT model fit indices. All indices below acceptable thresholds. Model did not converge.}
\label{tab:bifactor}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
\textbf{Index} & \textbf{Value} & \textbf{Threshold} \\
\midrule
Convergence & No & --- \\
RMSEA & 0.089 & $<0.06$ \\
CFI & 0.71 & $>0.90$ \\
TLI & 0.68 & $>0.90$ \\
SRMR & 0.112 & $<0.08$ \\
Heywood cases & 47 & 0 \\
Anchor items identified & 0 & $>0$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The bifactor IRT model (general factor + 57 subject-specific factors) fails to converge on the full matrix, with 47 Heywood cases (negative variance estimates).
All fit indices are far below acceptable thresholds.
No anchor items could be identified, terminating the IRT equating pipeline.
This negative result confirms that MMLU's item--model structure does not support confirmatory bifactor modeling at scale, consistent with the 98\% person-misfit finding.

\subsection{Experiment 10: Item Generation and Calibration}
\label{sec:itemgen}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{LLM-generated replacement item calibration. Difficulty correlation targets the 0.75 benchmark from the educational testing literature.}
\label{tab:itemgen}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
\textbf{Metric} & \textbf{Value} & \textbf{Target} \\
\midrule
$N$ items generated & 500 & --- \\
Difficulty $\rho$ (prompt-based) & 0.18 & 0.75 \\
Difficulty $\rho$ (student-sim) & 0.52 & 0.75 \\
Construct alignment (human) & 0.64 & $>0.80$ \\
Holdout panel $\rho$ & 0.41 & $>0.70$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Item generation falls below all calibration benchmarks.
Prompt-based difficulty targeting ($\rho=0.18$) is near the bottom of the literature range (0.10--0.45).
Student-simulation calibration ($\rho=0.52$) approaches but does not meet the 0.75 target.
Human-evaluated construct alignment (0.64) indicates 36\% of generated items fall outside the intended cognitive level or subject boundary.
Holdout model panel correlation (0.41) is insufficient to guarantee score equivalence.
The benchmark regeneration pipeline is not ready for deployment.

\subsection{Experiment 11: Cumulative Bayesian DIF}
\label{sec:bayesian}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Cumulative Bayesian DIF power over successive administrations (simulation, $\delta=1.0$). Target power: 0.70.}
\label{tab:bayesian}
\begin{tabular}{lc}
\toprule
\textbf{Administrations} & \textbf{Power} \\
\midrule
1 & 0.38 \\
2 & 0.49 \\
3 & 0.58 \\
4 & 0.64 \\
\midrule
Target & 0.70 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Simulation shows monotonic power accumulation across successive administrations, but the target of 0.70 is not reached after 4 administrations at $\delta=1.0$.
This is a simulation-only result with no real-data validation.
The stationarity assumption (benchmark items remain fixed across administrations) may be violated in the LLM setting where benchmarks themselves are subject to contamination over time.

\subsection{Experiment 12: Graceful Degradation Analysis}
\label{sec:degradation}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Benchmark validity metrics under progressive item replacement. $\rho$ = Spearman rank correlation of model rankings with original benchmark. $\alpha$ = Cronbach's reliability.}
\label{tab:degradation}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Replacement \%} & $\rho$ & $\alpha$ & \textbf{Coverage gap} \\
\midrule
0\% & 1.000 & 0.94 & --- \\
5\% & 0.994 & --- & --- \\
10\% & 0.986 & --- & --- \\
20\% & 0.961 & 0.88 & 3 subjects $>$30\% loss \\
50\% & 0.873 & --- & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Ranking correlation degrades faster than expected under item replacement.
At 20\% replacement, $\rho=0.961$ and reliability drops from 0.94 to 0.88.
Critically, simple item \emph{removal} (as in the diagnostic analysis of \S\ref{sec:exp1}) achieves $\rho\approx 0.997$---better than replacement---because the generated replacement items (\S\ref{sec:itemgen}) add noise rather than preserving measurement properties.
Three subjects lose $>$30\% of their items at 20\% replacement, creating uneven construct coverage.
Theoretical bounds are not tight enough to provide actionable replacement thresholds.

\subsection{Experiment 13: Item-Focused Trees}
\label{sec:ift}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{MH vs.\ Item-Focused Trees (IFT) flag comparison. IFT uses continuous temporal covariates rather than binary cohort splits.}
\label{tab:ift}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{Items flagged} & \textbf{Unique flags} \\
\midrule
MH-DIF & 3{,}918 (31.3\%) & 1{,}650 \\
IFT & 4{,}102 (32.8\%) & 1{,}834 \\
\midrule
Jaccard overlap & \multicolumn{2}{c}{0.48} \\
First split variable & \multicolumn{2}{c}{release\_date} \\
Interaction with model size & \multicolumn{2}{c}{$p<0.01$} \\
Temporal granularity & \multicolumn{2}{c}{quarterly splits} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Item-Focused Trees flag a comparable number of items (32.8\%) but with only 48\% overlap with MH, indicating substantial method disagreement.
IFT's continuous temporal treatment reveals quarterly granularity and a significant interaction with model size ($p<0.01$), suggesting that larger models (released later) contribute disproportionately to DIF.
The 52\% disagreement between methods raises questions about whether either method is detecting a robust item-level phenomenon or whether different modeling choices produce different artifact patterns.

%% ============================================================
%%  5  DISCUSSION AND LIMITATIONS
%% ============================================================
\section{Discussion and Limitations}

\paragraph{Summary of findings.}
We characterize MMLU's temporal measurement properties using MH-DIF at unprecedented scale.
31.3\% of items show large DIF between model generations, robust to multiple analytical variations.
The semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching ($\Delta$FPR$=$0.000) provides a reusable evaluation infrastructure.
Despite instability, MMLU rankings are resilient ($\rho\approx 0.997$), and DIF serves as a diagnostic lens for understanding inter-generational capability shifts.

\paragraph{IRT misspecification.}
The most significant limitation is the fundamental misspecification of unidimensional IRT for MMLU.
With 98\% of models showing $|L_z|>2$, the total score used for MH ability matching is a collapsed composite of 57-subject abilities.
Two models with identical total scores may have very different subject-level ability profiles, creating systematic ability confounding that inflates DIF detection rates.
The 31.3\% C\% figure must be interpreted in this context: it represents items flagged under 1D matching, and an unknown fraction may be spurious DIF driven by multidimensional ability differences rather than genuine item-level changes.

\paragraph{Bifactor failure.}
Our attempt to address misspecification via bifactor IRT modeling (\S\ref{sec:bifactor}) failed entirely (non-convergence, 47 Heywood cases).
This does not prove IRT is inapplicable to MMLU---exploratory MIRT or mixture IRT approaches may succeed---but it does confirm that confirmatory bifactor models are not viable at this scale.

\paragraph{Enrichment $\approx$ 1.0.}
DIF flags show no enrichment with web-overlap contamination labels.
We argue this is expected because web-overlap measures global exposure, not differential contamination.
An alternative interpretation is that DIF simply does not detect contamination.
The semi-synthetic results show DIF \emph{can} detect injected differential contamination, but the gap between semi-synthetic injection (binary flipping) and real-world contamination mechanisms remains.

\paragraph{FPR.}
DIF's FPR on Li-clean items is 0.318---nearly 1 in 3 clean items are flagged.
Cross-subject matching eliminates \emph{inflation} but not the baseline rate.
Without ground truth for item-level differential contamination, we cannot determine what fraction of the 31.3\% represents genuine item functioning changes vs.\ multidimensional confounding.

\paragraph{GSM1K limitations.}
The GSM1K correlation ($r=-0.478$, $N=33$) is suggestive but limited by sample size (BCa CI upper bound $|r|=0.155$) and potential difficulty confounding.

\paragraph{Comparison method scope.}
ConStat evaluation covers only 220 reformulated items; DVD covers 200 items $\times$ 12 models.
Ensemble fusion produces a null result.
Full-scale comparison would require substantially more resources.

\paragraph{Novelty.}
MH-DIF is a textbook method from 1988.
We apply it without modification to a new domain.
Our contributions are the empirical findings, the validation framework, and the ground-truth meta-critique---not algorithmic innovation.

\paragraph{Future directions.}
Three paths forward: (1) multidimensional IRT or MIMIC models that properly account for MMLU's 57-subject structure, (2) larger-scale cross-benchmark validation with more matched models, and (3) integration with perturbation-based methods for a multi-signal diagnostic framework.

%% ============================================================
%%  6  CONCLUSION
%% ============================================================
\section{Conclusion}

We apply Mantel--Haenszel DIF analysis to 5{,}227 models $\times$ 12{,}508 MMLU items, finding 31.3\% temporal item instability.
This finding is robust to threshold choice, multiple testing correction, and architecture composition, and is not explained by domain-level capability improvement ($\le$7.6\%).
We demonstrate that web-overlap contamination labels cannot validate item-level differential detectors and propose a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching ($\Delta$FPR$=$0.000).
Despite 31\% instability, MMLU rankings are massively redundant ($\rho\approx 0.997$), and DIF items serve as a diagnostic lens for inter-generational capability shifts rather than a quality filter.

The primary caveat is IRT misspecification: 98\% of models show poor person-fit under unidimensional IRT, and the true rate of genuine item-level instability may be lower than 31.3\%.
Resolving this requires multidimensional IRT methods that we have not yet successfully applied to MMLU-scale data.

