\maketitle

%% ============================================================
%%  ABSTRACT
%% ============================================================
\begin{abstract}
When we compare language model scores across time, we implicitly assume that benchmark items function identically for all model cohorts.
We test this assumption by applying Mantel--Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) analysis to the METABENCH response matrix (5{,}227 models $\times$ 12{,}508 MMLU items)---the largest-scale DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks to date.
We find that 31.3\% of items exhibit large DIF (ETS Category~C, $|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}|\!\ge\!1.5$) between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts, robust across thresholds (10--49\% at $|\Delta|\in[1.0,2.5]$), not explained by domain-level capability improvement ($\le$7.6\%), and architecturally homogeneous (99.98\% decoder-only).
We show that the field's standard web-overlap contamination labels cannot validate item-level differential detectors (enrichment$\approx$1.0) and propose a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching that eliminates false-positive inflation ($\Delta$FPR$=$0.000).
Despite 31\% instability, MMLU model rankings are massively redundant ($\rho\!\approx\!0.997$), and DIF items serve as a diagnostic lens---identifying \emph{which} capability dimensions shift across generations rather than flagging ``bad'' items.
Cross-benchmark replication on GSM8K (1{,}319 items $\times$ 6{,}702 models) confirms framework portability ($\Delta$TPR$=+$0.818) and shows DIF items resist contamination-based gains ($r\!=\!-0.478$, $p\!=\!0.005$).
\end{abstract}

%% ============================================================
%%  1  INTRODUCTION
%% ============================================================
\section{Introduction}

Benchmarks such as MMLU~\citep{hendrycks2021measuring} are the primary yardstick for comparing language models.
Temporal comparisons---``GPT-4 surpasses GPT-3.5 on MMLU''---rest on an untested assumption: that every item measures the same construct with the same difficulty for every model cohort.
Psychometrics has a mature tool for evaluating exactly this assumption: \emph{Differential Item Functioning} (DIF) analysis, which detects items whose difficulty changes across groups after controlling for overall ability~\citep{holland1988differential}.

We apply the Mantel--Haenszel (MH) DIF procedure---unchanged since 1988---to the METABENCH response matrix comprising 5{,}227 models and 12{,}508 MMLU items.
We do \emph{not} claim algorithmic novelty; MH-DIF is a textbook method.
Our contributions are empirical:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Temporal instability at scale.}
    31.3\% of MMLU items are ETS Category~C between 2023 and 2024-H1 cohorts.
    This is not a statistical artifact (random-split sanity: 0.18\%), not driven by capability improvement ($\le$7.6\%), and robust to threshold choice.
    \item \textbf{Ground-truth meta-critique.}
    Web-overlap contamination labels (e.g., Li et al.) measure global exposure, not differential contamination.
    Enrichment$\approx$1.0 is the expected outcome, not method failure.
    \item \textbf{Semi-synthetic validation framework.}
    $\Delta$TPR methodology with cross-subject matching ($\Delta$FPR$=$0.000).
    Portable to GSM8K ($\Delta$TPR$=+$0.818).
    \item \textbf{DIF as diagnostic lens.}
    Despite 31\% instability, rankings are stable ($\rho\!\approx\!0.997$).
    DIF items are the \emph{least} disruptive non-random removal strategy ($z\!=\!-9.5$ vs.\ accuracy-gap $z\!=\!-157$), isolating genuine differential functioning.
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{Key framing.}
Instability $\neq$ invalidity.
DIF flags items whose measurement properties changed---a diagnostic signal, not a quality verdict.
MMLU rankings survive 31\% item removal because the benchmark is massively redundant.
DIF tells us \emph{what changed}, not ``what is broken.''

%% ============================================================
%%  2  RELATED WORK
%% ============================================================
\section{Related Work}

\paragraph{LLM benchmark contamination.}
Web-overlap and $n$-gram methods~\citep{li2024task} identify publicly available items but do not assess \emph{differential} item functioning.
ConStat~\citep{constat2024} uses perturbation testing; Min-K\%~\citep{shi2024detecting} uses token-level statistics.
Both operate per-model, unlike DIF which is a group-level method.

\paragraph{Differential Item Functioning.}
The MH procedure~\citep{mantel1959statistical,holland1988differential} tests whether item difficulty differs between focal and reference groups after conditioning on total score.
ETS classifies items as A ($|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}|<1.0$), B ($1.0$--$1.5$), or C ($\ge\!1.5$).
DIF analysis has been standard in educational testing for decades.

\paragraph{IRT and LLM evaluation.}
Item Response Theory has been applied to LLM evaluation~\citep{irt_llm2024}, though IRT model fit for multidimensional benchmarks remains challenging.

%% ============================================================
%%  3  METHOD
%% ============================================================
\section{Method}

\subsection{Mantel--Haenszel DIF}

Given a binary response matrix $\mathbf{X}\in\{0,1\}^{N\times J}$ ($N$ models, $J$ items), we partition models into a \emph{reference} group (2023 cohort) and a \emph{focal} group (2024-H1 cohort).
For each item $j$ and total-score stratum $k$, the MH statistic tests the null hypothesis that the item--group odds ratio is 1.
The effect size $\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}$ (in ETS delta units) quantifies the magnitude.
Items with $|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}|\ge 1.5$ and statistically significant MH $\chi^2$ are classified as ETS Category~C.

\paragraph{IRT fit caveat.}
MH conditions on total score as an ability proxy.
On MMLU (57 subjects), the unidimensional IRT model is misspecified: 98\% of models show person-fit $|L_z|>2$.
Total score collapses a multidimensional ability space into a single composite.
This is a known limitation throughout the paper.

\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation Framework}

To evaluate DIF detection power with known ground truth, we inject controlled contamination into the real METABENCH response matrix:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Select $n_{\mathrm{clean}}$ items with no DIF signal as ``clean'' items.
    \item For each ``injected'' item, flip a fraction $p$ of focal-group incorrect responses to correct, simulating differential contamination.
    \item Run MH-DIF on the modified matrix and measure $\Delta\text{TPR} = \text{TPR}_{\text{injected}} - \text{TPR}_{\text{baseline}}$.
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{Cross-subject matching.}
Standard MH uses total score (all subjects) as matching variable.
When contamination affects the matching variable itself, FPR is inflated.
We propose using total score on \emph{other} subjects as the matching variable, eliminating this confound.

\subsection{Comparison Methods}

\paragraph{ConStat perturbation testing.}
We reformulate 220 MMLU items and measure detection rate via paired accuracy drop.

\paragraph{DVD generation variance.}
For 12 models $\times$ 200 items $\times$ 50 generations, we detect bimodal response variance as a contamination signal.

\paragraph{Ensemble fusion.}
Majority vote and weighted fusion of DIF + ConStat + DVD signals.

%% ============================================================
%%  4  EXPERIMENTS
%% ============================================================
\section{Experiments}

\subsection{Experiment 1: Temporal DIF on MMLU}
\label{sec:exp1}

\paragraph{Setup.}
5{,}227 models $\times$ 12{,}508 MMLU items from METABENCH.
Reference: models released before 2024; Focal: 2024-H1.

